setupapi: accept SP_COPY_NODECOMP without an unsupported style error.

Windows SetupAPI defines SP_COPY_NODECOMP (0x10) to specify that the source
file should be copied verbatim without attempting decompression. Wine's
do_file_copyW already performs a direct copy via copy_file() / CopyFileExW(),
so the requested behaviour is already satisfied. Flagging SP_COPY_NODECOMP
as an unsupported style produces noisy ERR lines in session logs during
wineboot --init and various runtime installers, causing callers and log
parsers to treat the operation as failed.

diff --git a/dlls/setupapi/queue.c b/dlls/setupapi/queue.c
index bea68177b89..73bb4882e1f 100644
--- a/dlls/setupapi/queue.c
+++ b/dlls/setupapi/queue.c
@@ -1162,7 +1162,14 @@ static BOOL do_file_copyW( LPCWSTR source, LPCWSTR target, DWORD style,
             docopy = FALSE;
         }
     }
-    if (style & (SP_COPY_NODECOMP | SP_COPY_LANGUAGEAWARE | SP_COPY_FORCE_IN_USE |
+    /* Vessel: SP_COPY_NODECOMP (0x10) instructs file copy operations to copy the
+     * file verbatim without attempting decompression. Wine's copy_file() already
+     * copies directly via CopyFileExW() without decompression, so the flag's
+     * semantics are fully satisfied. Flagging it as an unsupported style logs
+     * spurious ERR messages during INF-driven setups (e.g. wineboot --init and
+     * runtime installers).
+     */
+    if (style & (SP_COPY_LANGUAGEAWARE | SP_COPY_FORCE_IN_USE |
                  SP_COPY_NOSKIP | SP_COPY_WARNIFSKIP))
     {
         ERR("Unsupported style(s) 0x%lx\n",style);
